USER: how do i know if my baby has jaundice? ASSISTANT: Jaundice, sometimes called “the yellow disease”, is the yellowing of the skin and the whites of the eyes.  It’s typically a symptom of infection, but it can also occur in babies whose systems aren’t yet making enough bilirubin.  In most cases, a doctor or a nurse can diagnose jaundice by examining your baby’s skin. USER: Any other advice? ASSISTANT: Jaundice is harmless and usually clears up quickly.  If your baby has jaundice, it’s best not to use any remedies, and to let a doctor or nurse take care of it.  You can do your best to keep your baby comfortable by following a few simple guidelines:
  • keep your baby’s room as dark as possible, but not too dark;
  • make sure your baby gets plenty of rest, sleep, and sunshine;
  • keep your baby warm. USER: why do i need to keep the room dark? ASSISTANT: It’s important for your baby’s health that the light inside his or her room isn’t too bright.  So please keep the curtains closed at all times to make sure your baby isn’t exposed to too much light.  This can prevent a sudden change in your baby’s USER: I thought you said the baby needed sunlight

